

--> What is Microservices Architecture?
        Microservices architecture is a software design approach where an application is divided into small, independent services, each responsible for a single business capability.
        Each service runs in its own process
        Communicates with other services via APIs (HTTP/REST or messaging)
        Can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently
        👉 Example: User Service, Order Service, Payment Service as separate applications.

--> Monolithic vs Microservices
        Monolithic Architecture
            Entire application is one single unit
            All modules share the same codebase and database
            One small change requires rebuilding and redeploying the whole app
            Failure in one module can affect the entire system
        Microservices Architecture
            Application is split into multiple small services
            Each service has its own codebase and database
            Services can be deployed independently
            Failure in one service does not crash others

        👉 In short:
        Monolith = tightly coupled
        Microservices = loosely coupled

--> Key Characteristics of Microservices
        Independent Deployment – Services can be deployed without affecting others
        Scalability – Scale only the service that needs more resources
        Fault Isolation – One service failure won’t break the entire system
        Decentralized Data Management – Each service manages its own database

--> Advantages of Microservices
        Better scalability (scale only required services)
        Faster development (multiple teams work independently)
        Flexibility (different tech stacks per service)
        Easier maintenance due to smaller codebases

--> Challenges of Microservices
        Increased complexity (many services to manage)
        Network latency (inter-service communication over network)
        Data consistency issues (distributed databases)
        Monitoring & debugging is harder than monolith

--> Common Microservices Components
        API Gateway – Single entry point for all client requests
        Service Registry – Keeps track of service locations (e.g., Eureka)
        Config Server – Centralized configuration management
        Load Balancer – Distributes traffic across service instances

--> Communication Types
        Synchronous Communication
            REST APIs using HTTP
            Client waits for response
            Example: Order Service calling User Service via REST
        Asynchronous Communication
            Messaging systems (Kafka, RabbitMQ)
            No direct waiting for response
            Used for high-throughput, event-driven systems

